## 4. The Diagnosis: Why Your Content Isn't Ranking

### 4.1 Client-side rendering is hiding your pages from Google (primary cause)

When the audit fetched your inner pages as a crawler would (raw HTTP, no browser), **every URL returned the exact same HTML shell** — the same 22.5 KB document with the homepage title "LoadSaathi – AI Truck Load & Return Load Marketplace in India" — for `/routes/rourkela-to-ranchi`, `/blog/rourkela-to-ranchi-trucking-rates`, and `/blog` alike. There is no unique title, no meta description, and no per-page JSON-LD in the server response.

> Google's own documentation states that JavaScript rendering is a second-pass process: Googlebot first indexes the raw HTML, then queues the page for JS rendering later in a separate wave. If the raw HTML is nearly empty, pages can sit unranked for weeks or months, and for low-authority new domains the rendering queue deprioritizes content.

This means Google's first-pass index currently sees 112 URLs that all look like thin copies of your homepage. That pattern is a classic de-ranking signal. Your competitors (Trukky, TruckGuru, Wheelseye) all serve **server-rendered, static HTML** where the route page, the rate page, and the blog post exist as real HTML with unique titles and rich content — which is exactly why Trukky owns positions #1 and #2 for "Rourkela to Ranchi truck rate" with almost no authority advantage over you.

### 4.2 Low domain authority against entrenched incumbents (secondary cause)

For each keyword, the top results are either directories with decades of link equity (Justdial, IndiaMART), established national players (Trukky, TruckGuru, Vahak, Wheelseye, FR8, returnloads.in), or authority tax sites (ClearTax, CBIC). Your domain is young and its backlink profile is thin. This makes the technical fix even more important: you cannot out-muscle these domains with links yet, but you *can* out-execute them technically and on long-tail keywords where they are weak.

### 4.3 Intent mismatches on two keyword families

Two SERPs showed that even where you might rank, the *searcher's intent* diverges from what you assume. "Transport service in Rourkela to Ranchi" is dominated by **bus booking results** — Google reads the query as passenger travel, not freight. "Digital freight marketplace India" is a **homepage SERP** where only direct marketplace landing pages compete; blog articles will never win it. Both require different page types (a dedicated city-to-city service landing page with freight-first copy, and category landing pages rather than blog posts).

